    "issued_at": "2026-09-06T11:25:00+00:00",
    "product_id": "b1127367-9350-4193-94c5-fe49ad3968b6",
    "text": "\n000\nFXUS65 KVEF 061125\nAFDVEF\n\nArea Forecast Discussion\nNational Weather Service Las Vegas NV\n425 AM PDT Sun Sep 6 2026\n\n.KEY MESSAGES...\n\n* A band of showers with a chance of embedded thunderstorms will \n  form along Interstate 15 this morning and afternoon.\n\n* Drier conditions expected for Labor Day, with decreasing \n  confidence in monsoonal thunderstorms through the week.\n\n&&\n\n\n.DISCUSSION...Today through Saturday.\n\nA surge of moisture reminiscent of a winter-type system is pushing \nnorthward in a line from San Diego through Needles into northern \nArizona early this morning thanks to moisture from Hurricane Marie. \nThe upper-level trough responsible for ushering this moisture into \nthe Desert Southwest will also guide a shortwave and an associated \n115 kt 150mb jet streak into the region. The combination of these \nfeatures will result in a line of showers (and potentially embedded \nthunderstorms) forming southwest-to-northeast across our forecast \narea, generally along Interstate 15. This band of precipitation is \ncurrently forecast to be quite narrow, with northwest and southeast \ncorners of our forecast area remaining dry - and even, for some, \nexperiencing mostly sunny skies. That said, minor shifts in the \nposition of the moisture flux could result in previously forecasted \ndry locations observing measurable rainfall. Mostly cloudy skies \nthroughout the Mojave Desert and southeastern Great Basin this \nmorning will prevent much in the way of SBCAPE through the \nafternoon. As a result, lift will be largely dependent on \norographics - meaning, the greatest chance of lightning and/or heavy \nrainfall will be in the higher terrain. Looking at chances of \nreceiving 1 inch of rainfall over the next 24 hours, the HREF \ncontinues to paint 60-70 percent over the higher elevations of Mt. \nCharleston and 30-40 percent over the mountains of eastern Lincoln \nCounty. Given the trajectory of this moisture, the Las Vegas Valley \nis likely to experience a substantial amount of rain-shadowing, \nresulting in much lower forecast precipitation amounts (generally \nbetween 0.15 and 0.30 inches). That said, should the trajectory gain \na greater south-to-north component, moisture would funnel directly \ninto the Las Vegas Valley, resulting in greater rainfall amounts \n(the 90th percentile approaches 0.50 inches). The primary concern \ntoday will be the training of stronger cells embedded in the \nprecipitation band resulting in nuisance ponding or even flash \nflooding, particularly in locations like Pahrump, which will \nexperience substantial runoff from the southwestern side of the \nSpring Mountains. In general, the flash flood threat is expected to \nbe very isolated, if any at all, due to the stratiform nature of \nthis precipitation. Precipitation will gradually subside late-\nafternoon into the evening.\n\nThere is decreasing confidence in the return of monsoonal \nprecipitation this week, as the Four Corners high shifts slightly \nwestward. PWATs in excess of 1 inch will remain trapped beneath the \nridge, with little instability aside from daytime heating. As a \nresult, cannot completely rule out isolated convection, particularly \nin the mountains. Through the work week, temperatures will increase \nthanks to rising heights, with the center of the high overhead by \nThursday.\n&&\n\n\n.AVIATION...For Harry Reid...For the 12Z Forecast Package... Cloud \ncover will continue to increase through the morning, with bases \nlowering to less than 10 kft AGL by mid-morning. Shower chances \nincrease around 12Z, with -RA prevailing by 15Z through mid-\nafternoon. With shower activity overhead, expect CIGs dropping as \nlow as 5 kft with a slight (10-20 percent) chance of visibilities \nreducing below 6 SM. Precipitation chances drop off around 03Z, with \nimproving ceilings into the overnight period. Otherwise, expect \nelevated south-southwest winds in the afternoon, with a diurnal \npattern on Monday. Temperatures will remain below 100F through at \nleast Tuesday.\n\nFor the rest of southern Nevada, northwest Arizona and southeast \nCalifornia...For the 12Z Forecast Package...Cloud cover will \ncontinue to increase through the morning, with bases lowering to \nless than 10 kft AGL by mid-morning, except for KBIH where FEW high \nclouds are expected. KIFP and KEED can expect light shower activity \nfor the next couple of hours before the rain band pushes northward \nto impact KDAG and the Las Vegas Valley TAF sites. Shower chances \ncontinue for the Mojave Desert through the afternoon, with chances \ntapering off quickly after 03Z with improving ceilings. Otherwise, \nexpect breezy south-southwest winds with the exception of the \nColorado River Valley TAF sites where gusty north winds will pick up \nafter 18Z.\n&&\n\n\n.SPOTTER INFORMATION STATEMENT...Spotters are encouraged to report\nany significant weather or impacts according to standard operating\nprocedures.\n\n&&\n\n$$\n\nDISCUSSION...Soulat\nAVIATION...Soulat\n\nFor more forecast information...see us on our webpage:\nhttps://weather.gov/lasvegas or follow us on Facebook and Twitter\n",
